          "court": "Court of Appeals of North Carolina",
          "year": 2005,
          "date_decided": "2005-07-19",
          "holding": "The Court of Appeals upheld the ex parte order directing the mother to cease interfering with the DSS investigation but held that the trial court lacked jurisdiction to award sole temporary custody to the father because no nonsecure custody request had been made and no abuse, neglect, or dependency petition had been filed.",
          "relevance": "It confirms the court's exclusive original jurisdiction over petitions alleging obstruction of a required investigation.",
          "quote": "a request for nonsecure custody must be made and a petition must be filed before the court may issue an order for nonsecure custody",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/7473109/in-re-kcg/",
